From cf10ba7c61337400c9b3b8b2e9ff0a208c73f472 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 08:53:46 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 10889/11362] Fix two failed daily timers: save-run poll
 shorter than the save-run, news-watch timeout shorter than the check

carnot-arc-daily-prep.service failed exit 1 on every unattended run. The
save-run poll was 24 x 15s (6 min); the v53 save-run took ~770s on Kaggle.
The poll always expired first, wrote the ambiguous save_run='?', and the
prep exited 1 while Kaggle finished fine minutes later -- the false alarm
already hand-verified on kernel versions 9, 22, 27, and 37. Fix: extract
poll_save_run() with a 3600s budget at 30s intervals, injectable
sleep/clock for tests, and an explicit still_running_after_<N>s verdict on
expiry (REQ-HARNESS-6054 + 4 regression tests). ops/arc-daily-prep-status.json
corrected to v53 ground truth per the v9/v37 precedent (save-run COMPLETE,
submission.parquet present, 2648 bytes, 1x4). The unit was deliberately NOT
started: its default mode re-versions the Kaggle dataset and re-pushes the
public kernel, which is operator-gated. Verified instead by --dry-run with
the unit's exact interpreter and cwd (exit 0) plus read-only kernels
status/output checks.

arc-news-watch.service failed on 7 of its last 12 daily runs with
CHECK_TIMED_OUT: the codex web-search check exceeded its 300s timeout,
while successful checks take 4-8 minutes wall. Fix: raise the timeout to
900s and name the budget in the timeout log message. Rejected: swallowing
the timeout (exit 0) -- the unit should stay red when the check really
fails.

Also diagnosed why journalctl --user had no entries for either failure:
the conductor logs full file diffs to the user journal, rotating a 6.4MB
journal file every ~5 minutes against a ~44MB cap, so retention is ~40
minutes. Not fixed here; proposed StandardOutput=append: for the conductor
unit or raised journald caps (root) instead.

